<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles D. B. King</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles Dunbar Burgess King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles D. B. King was the 17th president of Liberia, in office from 1920 to 1930. As secretary of state he represented Liberia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> of 1919, and as president he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> concession of 1926. When a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> commission found in 1930 that senior officials had been involved in shipping forced labourers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>, he resigned with his vice-president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king.jpg" alt="Charles D. B. King" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Léon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_Charles_Dunbar_Burgess_King.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
